{"cuisine/tianjin-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/tianjin-cuisine","n":"Tianjin cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Tianjin cuisine (Tientsin cuisine), also known as Jin cuisine, refers to the native cooking styles of Tianjin, the largest port city in Northern China. Though heavily influenced by Beijing cuisine, Tianjin cuisine differs by being more focused on seafood. It is categorized by its freshness, saltiness and soft and crispy textures. Cooking methods include grilling, simmering, sautéing and steaming. With more than 300 years of history, the development of the Tianjin cuisine was highly dependent on the diet of boatmen and the salt trades due to its geographical location. Tianjin Food Street is a place where cross-cultural Chinese dishes may be found. Popular dishes include Eight Great Bowls, Four Great Stews, Tianjing goubuli, and Four Winter Delicacies, among others. Eight Great Bowls is a combination of eight different meat dishes. The Four Great Stews refers to a very large number of stews, including chicken, duck, seafood, beef, and mutton. Tianjin also has several famous snack items. Goubuli (狗不理包子) is a classic steamed stuffed bun (baozi) that is well-known throughout China. Guifaxiang (桂发祥麻花) is a traditional brand of mahua (twisted dough sticks)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"tianjin-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tianjin_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/north-east-indian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/north-east-indian-cuisine","n":"North East Indian cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Northeast India, officially the North Eastern Region (NER), is the easternmost region of India representing both a geographic and political administrative division of the country. It comprises eight states: Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, and Tripura (commonly known as the \"Seven Sisters\"), and the state of Sikkim. Northeast India is one of the most linguistically diverse regions in the world."],["The region has India's international border of 5,182 kilometres (3,220 mi) with five neighbouring countries: China to the north, Myanmar to the east, Bangladesh to the southwest, Nepal to the west, and Bhutan to the northwest. It comprises an area of 262,184 square kilometres (101,230 sq mi), almost 8 per cent of that of India and has a population of 45,772,188, almost 4 per cent that of India. The Siliguri Corridor connects the region to other parts of India."],["Northeast India is very rich in mineral wealth; India's first oil well was dug in Northeast India in 1865, in Digboi in Assam. The region houses one of the notable ophiolites in the world."],["The region has been highly strategic and it witnessed one of the fiercest battles in the Second World War, the Battle of Imphal and Kohima (1944) where the Japanese Forces and the Allied Forces were engaged in a pitched battle that saw thousands of casualties on both sides."],["The states of North Eastern Region are officially recognised under the North Eastern Council (NEC), constituted in 1971 as the acting agency for the development of the north eastern states. Sikkim became a member of the North Eastern Council in 2002. India's Look-East connectivity projects connect Northeast India to East Asia and ASEAN."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"north-east-indian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_East_Indian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Brahmos Foodie","ref":"site/brahmosfoodie-blogspot-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"brahmosfoodie.blogspot.com","note":"Blog of recipes from and inspired by North-East India.","url":"https://brahmosfoodie.blogspot.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Essential North-East Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/the-essential-north-east-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Hoihnu Hauzel","note":"Recipes from the seven northeastern states, published by Penguin India.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within North East Indian cooking","count":6,"links":[["Assamese cuisine","cuisine/assamese-cuisine"],["Manipuri cuisine","cuisine/manipuri-cuisine"],["Mizo cuisine","cuisine/mizo-cuisine"],["Naga cuisine","cuisine/naga-cuisine"],["Sikkimese cuisine","cuisine/sikkimese-cuisine"],["Tripuri cuisine","cuisine/tripuri-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/90/Northeast_india.png/500px-Northeast_india.png","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Renzut","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northeast_india.png"}},"ingredient/sulguni":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/sulguni","n":"Sulguni","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Sulguni is a brined Georgian cheese from the Samegrelo region. It has a sour, moderately salty flavor, a dimpled texture, and an elastic consistency; these attributes are the result of the process used, as is the source of its nickname \"pickled cheese\". Its color ranges from white to pale yellow. Suluguni is often deep-fried, which masks its odor. It is often served in wedges. Suluguni has been protected as a geographical indication by Georgia since 24 January 2012."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-sulguni"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulguni"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Achma","dish/achma"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Khachapuri","ingredient/khachapuri"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/82/Sulguni_slice.JPG/500px-Sulguni_slice.JPG","w":500,"h":356,"by":"Jonathan Cardy","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sulguni_slice.JPG"}},"ingredient/palm-sugar":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/palm-sugar","n":"Palm sugar","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 29 dishes across 7 cuisines","intro":[["Palm sugar is a sweetener derived from any variety of palm tree. Palm sugar may be qualified by the type of palm, as in coconut palm sugar. While sugars from different palms may have slightly different compositions, all are processed similarly and can be used interchangeably."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-palm-sugar"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palm_sugar"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":7,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur-ketan-hitam"],["Cendol","dish/cendol"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Getuk goreng","dish/getuk-goreng"],["Gudeg","dish/gudeg"],["Kerak telor","dish/kerak-telor"],["Klepon","dish/klepon"],["Kolak (food)","dish/kolak-food"],["Kue rangi","dish/kue-rangi"],["Lampet","dish/lampet"],["Lupis (food)","dish/lupis-food"],["Madumongso","dish/madumongso"],["Nasi gandul","dish/nasi-gandul"],["Pempek","dish/pempek"],["Roti gambang","dish/roti-gambang"],["Rujak soto","dish/rujak-soto"],["