Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Pineapple","ingredient/pineapple"]]}]},"dish/cacio-e-pepe":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/cacio-e-pepe","n":"Cacio e pepe","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Lazian or Roman","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Cacio e pepe is a pasta dish typical of Lazio, Italy. It contains grated pecorino romano and black pepper with tonnarelli or spaghetti. Achieving a smooth and creamy texture during preparation requires special attention, as the cheese is prone to becoming lumpy."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-cacio-e-pepe"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cacio_e_pepe"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Lazian or Roman","cuisine/roman-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Pasta","ingredient/pasta"],["Pecorino romano","ingredient/pecorino-romano"],["Pecorino Romano PDO","ingredient/pecorino-romano-pdo"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Spaghetti","ingredient/spaghetti"],["Spaghetti alla chitarra","ingredient/spaghetti-alla-chitarra"]]},{"h":"More from Lazian or Roman","links":[["Abbacchio","dish/abbacchio"],["Coda alla vaccinara","dish/coda-alla-vaccinara"],["Pinsa (bread)","dish/pinsa-bread"],["Roman pizza","dish/roman-pizza"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/99/Cacio_e_pepe.jpg/500px-Cacio_e_pepe.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Popo le Chien","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cacio_e_pepe.jpg"}},"dish/fafda":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/fafda","n":"Fafda","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Fafda is a popular Indian snack native to the western state of Gujarat. Fafda is a type of papad and is part of a category of snack food called farsan."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-fafda"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fafda"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Swasthi's Recipes","ref":"site/indianhealthyrecipes-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"indianhealthyrecipes.com","note":"Swasthi Shreekanth's long-running blog of everyday Indian home cooking from across the regions.","url":"https://www.indianhealthyrecipes.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Hari Ghotra","ref":"site/harighotra-co-uk","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"harighotra.co.uk","note":"UK-based cook Hari Ghotra's recipe library and videos of home-style Indian dishes.","url":"https://www.harighotra.co.uk/indian-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Indian cuisine","links":[["Akuri","dish/akuri"],["Aloo paratha","dish/aloo-paratha"],["Amba (condiment)","dish/amba-condiment"],["Baingan bharta","dish/baingan-bharta"],["Bombay potato","dish/bombay-potato"],["Butter chicken","dish/butter-chicken"],["Chaat masala","dish/chaat-masala"],["Chicken Chettinad","dish/chicken-chettinad"],["Dahi vada","dish/dahi-vada"],["Dal dhokli","dish/dal-dhokli"],["Dal makhani","dish/dal-makhani"],["Falooda","dish/falooda"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Handvo","dish/handvo"],["Hyderabadi haleem","dish/hyderabadi-haleem"],["Idli","dish/idli"],["Indian restaurant","dish/indian-restaurant"],["Kachumber","dish/kachumber"],["Kidyo","dish/kidyo"],["Kulfi","dish/kulfi"],["Masala chai","dish/masala-chai"],["Mirchi bada","dish/mirchi-bada"],["Mokthuk","dish/mokthuk"],["Naan","dish/naan"],["Osmania biscuit","dish/osmania-biscuit"],["Panipuri","dish/panipuri"],["Pathia","dish/pathia"],["Rabri","dish/rabri"],["Shemai","dish/shemai"],["Tandoori chicken","dish/tandoori-chicken"],["Tandoori roti","dish/tandoori-roti"],["Thali","dish/thali"],["Zarda (food)","dish/zarda-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/30/Fafda.JPG/500px-Fafda.JPG","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Shubhamj29","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fafda.JPG"}},"ingredient/thyme":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/thyme","n":"Thyme","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Thyme () is a culinary herb consisting of the dried aerial parts of some members of the genus Thymus of flowering plants in the mint family Lamiaceae. Thymes are native to Mediterranean countries in North Africa, Levant and Southern Europe. Thymes have culinary, medicinal, and ornamental uses. The species most commonly cultivated and used for culinary purposes is Thymus vulgaris, native to Southeast Europe."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-thyme"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thyme"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Belgian cuisine","cuisine/belgian-cuisine",[["Flemish stew","dish/flemish-stew"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Ratatouille","dish/ratatouille"]]],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Guanciale","dish/guanciale"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Ratatouille","dish/ratatouille"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Bay leaf","ingredient/bay-leaf"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Beer","ingredient/beer"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Pork jowl","ingredient/pork-jowl"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Aubergine","ingredient/aubergine"],["Basil","ingredient/basil"],["Bay leaves","ingredient/bay-leaves"],["Bell pepper","ingredient/bell-pepper"],["Courgette","ingredient/courgette"],["Fennel","ingredient/fennel"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ea/Thyme-Bundle.jpg/500px-Thyme-Bundle.jpg","w":500,"h":274,"by":"Evan-Amos","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thyme-Bundle.jpg"}},"ingredient/rendang":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/rendang","n":"Rendang","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Rendang is a stir-fried meat dish or dry curry made of meat stewed in coconut milk and spices, widely popular across Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore, Brunei, and the southern parts of the Philippines, where each version is considered local cuisine. It refers to both a cooking method of frying and the dish cooked in that way. The process involves slowly cooking meat in spiced coconut milk in an uncovered pot or pan until the oil separates, allowing the dish to fry in its own sauce, coating the meat in a rich, flavorful glaze."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rendang"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rendang"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Javanese cuisine","cuisine/javanese-cuisine",[["Nasi ambeng","dish/nasi-ambeng"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Perkedel","ingredient/perkedel"],["Rempeyek","ingredient/rempeyek"],["Sambal","ingredient/sambal"],["Tempeh","ingredient/tempeh"],["Urap","ingredient/urap"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/70/Rendang_daging_sapi_asli_Padang.JPG/500px-Rendang_daging_sapi_asli_Padang.JPG","w":500,"h":335,"by":"Midori","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rendang_daging_sapi_asli_Padang.JPG"}},"dish/nasi-kebuli":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/nasi-kebuli","n":"Nasi kebuli","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Nasi kebuli is an Indonesian variation of pilaf.  It consists of rice cooked in goat meat broth, goat milk, and clarified butter (most often ghee). It is popular among the Arab community in Indonesia (most prominent in Arab-descent Betawi in Greater Jakarta). Nasi kebuli was influenced by Arab culture and its origin can be traced to Middle eastern cuisine, especially Yemeni Arabian influence (mandi rice or kabsa), Indian cuisine influence (biryani rice), and Afghan influence (kabuli palaw)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-nasi-kebuli"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nasi_kebuli"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":10,"links":[["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Caraway","ingredient/caraway"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Ghee","ingredient/ghee"],["Goat","ingredient/goat"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","ref":"site/dailycookingquest-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Botok","dish/botok"],["Brem","dish/brem"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur-ketan-hitam"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-food"],["Cendol","dish/cendol"],["Cenil","dish/cenil"],["Cilok","dish/cilok"],["Cimpa","dish/cimpa"],["Corn fritter","dish/corn-fritter"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Dadiah","dish/dadiah"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Emping","dish/emping"],["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Es doger","dish/es-doger"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Geplak","dish/geplak"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/37/Nasi_Kebuli_Jakarta.JPG/500px-Nasi_Kebuli_Jakarta.JPG","w":500,"h":389,"by":"Gunawan Kartapranata","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nasi_Kebuli_Jakarta.JPG"}},"dish/palm-nut-soup":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/palm-nut-soup","n":"Palm nut soup","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#e46a3f","intro":[["Palm nut soup or banga is a soup made from palm fruit common in Cameroon, Sierra Leone, Ghana, Nigeria, Democratic Republic of Congo and Ivory Coast. The soup is made from a palm cream or palm nut base with stewed marinated meats, smoked dried fish, and aromatics. It is often eaten with starch, eba, fufu, omotuo, banku, fonio."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-palm-nut-soup"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palm_nut_soup"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Cameroonian cuisine","cuisine/cameroonian-cuisine"],["Nigerian cuisine","cuisine/nigerian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Crayfish","ingredient/crayfish"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Precious Core","ref":"site/preciouscore-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"preciouscore.com","note":"Recipe blog by Precious Nkeih centered on Cameroonian dishes, with a companion YouTube channel.","url":"https://www.preciouscore.com/?p=22","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Immaculate Bites","ref":"site/africanbites-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"africanbites.com","note":"African and Caribbean recipes from Cameroon born cook Imma, with many Cameroonian dishes.","url":"https://www.africanbites.com/about","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Kitchen Butterfly","ref":"site/kitchenbutterfly-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"kitchenbutterfly.com","note":"Long-running blog by Nigerian food writer and historian Ozoz Sokoh, with Nigerian and New Nigerian recipes.","url":"https://www.kitchenbutterfly.com/about-2","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Sisi Yemmie","ref":"site/sisiyemmie-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"sisiyemmie.com","note":"Nigerian home cooking recipes and meal plans, paired with the SisiYemmieTV YouTube channel.","url":"https://sisiyemmie.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/bc/Eba_served_with_Fresh_fish_banga_soup_in_a_clay_pot.jpg/500px-Eba_served_with_Fresh_fish_banga_soup_in_a_clay_pot.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Justdifference","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eba_served_with_Fresh_fish_banga_soup_in_a_clay_pot.jpg"}},"ingredient/edible-mushrooms":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/edible-mushrooms","n":"Edible mushrooms","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 6 cuisines","intro":[["Edible mushrooms are the fleshy fruit bodies of numerous species of properly identified and prepared fungi. Edibility may be defined by criteria including their palatability and absence of dangerous mycotoxins. Edible mushrooms are consumed for their nutritional and culinary value, often either being cultivated or harvested wild. Easily cultivated and common wild mushrooms are often available in markets; those that are more difficult to obtain may be collected on a smaller scale."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-edible-mushrooms"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edible_mushrooms"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":6,"groups":[["Belarusian cuisine","cuisine/belarusian-cuisine",[["Solyanka","dish/solyanka"]]],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Tekwan","dish/tekwan"]]],["Palembang cuisine","cuisine/palembang-cuisine",[["Tekwan","dish/tekwan"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Solyanka","dish/solyanka"]]],["Soviet cuisine","cuisine/soviet-cuisine",[["Solyanka","dish/solyanka"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Solyanka","dish/solyanka"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Boletus edulis","ingredient/boletus-edulis"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Dill","ingredient/dill"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Lemon","ingredient/lemon"],["Olea europaea","ingredient/olea-europaea"],["Olive","ingredient/olive"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Pickled cucumber","ingredient/pickled-cucumber"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Smetana (dairy product)","ingredient/smetana-dairy-product"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Egg white","ingredient/egg-white"],["Fish cake","ingredient/fish-cake"],["Fishcake","ingredient/fishcake"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/18/Edible_fungi_in_basket_2009_G1_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Edible_fungi_in_basket_2009_G1_%28cropped%29.jpg","w":500,"h":264,"by":"George Chernilevsky","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edible_fungi_in_basket_2009_G1_(cropped).jpg"}},"dish/thepla":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/thepla","n":"Thepla","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Gujarati cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Thepla is a soft Indian flatbread typical of Gujarati cuisine While extremely popular across Gujarat, it is especially common amongst the Jain community."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-thepla"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thepla"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Gujarati cuisine","cuisine/gujarati-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Atta","ingredient/atta"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Spice Up The Curry (Andhra)","ref":"site/spiceupthecurry-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"spiceupthecurry.com","note":"Gujarati section of Kanan Patel's Indian vegetarian recipe blog.","url":"https://www.spiceupthecurry.com/category/recipes-by-region/gujarat/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Gujarati cuisine","links":[["Dhokla","dish/dhokla"],["Undhiyu","dish/undhiyu"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/74/Thepla_main.jpg/500px-Thepla_main.jpg","w":500,"h":404,"by":"Rocky231293","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thepla_main.jpg"}},"dish/tsukudani":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/tsukudani","n":"Tsukudani","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Tsukudani is thinly sliced seafood, meat or seaweed that has been simmered in soy sauce and mirin. As a flavorful accompaniment to plain rice, tsukudani is made salty enough to not go bad, allowing high osmotic pressure to preserve the ingredients from microbial spoilage similarly to other types of pickles. Its name originates from Tsukudajima, the island (in present-day Chūō, Tokyo) where it was first made in the Edo period. Many kinds of tsukudani are sold, and common ingredients include kelp, short-neck clam, young lancefish, and nori. Traditionally made tsukudani is preservable and has been favored as a storable side dish in Japanese kitchens since the Edo period."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-tsukudani"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsukudani"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","ref":"site/justonecookbook-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's long-running blog and videos on Japanese home cooking.","url":"https://justonecookbook.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"RecipeTin Japan","ref":"site/japan-recipetineats-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"japan.recipetineats.com","note":"Japanese home recipes from Yumiko, a Japan-born cook, sister site to RecipeTin Eats.","url":"https://japan.recipetineats.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Japanese cuisine","links":[["Bergh–Stoutenburgh House","dish/bergh-stoutenburgh-house"],["Chikuwa","dish/chikuwa"],["Curry bread","dish/curry-bread"],["Donburi","dish/donburi"],["Fukamushicha","dish/fukamushicha"],["Gyokuro","dish/gyokuro"],["Hitsumabushi","dish/hitsumabushi"],["Horumonyaki","dish/horumonyaki"],["Iekei ramen","dish/iekei-ramen"],["Inago no tsukudani","dish/inago-no-tsukudani"],["Itamae","dish/itamae"],["Izakaya","dish/izakaya"],["Japanese cheesecake","dish/japanese-cheesecake"],["Japanese noodles","dish/japanese-noodles"],["Japanese restaurant","dish/japanese-restaurant"],["Japanese tea","dish/japanese-tea"],["Jingisukan","dish/jingisukan"],["Kaki furai","dish/kaki-furai"],["Kamaboko","dish/kamaboko"],["Kōji (food)","dish/koji-food"],["Kusa mochi","dish/kusa-mochi"],["Kushikatsu","dish/kushikatsu"],["List of Japanese restaurants","dish/list-of-japanese-restaurants"],["Makizushi","dish/makizushi"],["Miso","dish/miso"],["Miso soup","dish/miso-soup"],["Monjayaki","dish/monjayaki"],["Nikujaga","dish/nikujaga"],["Nimono","dish/nimono"],["Nori","dish/nori"],["Oden","dish/oden"],["Onigiri","dish/onigiri"],["Osechi","dish/osechi"],["Oyakodon","dish/oyakodon"],["Rainbow roll","dish/rainbow-roll"],["Ramen","dish/ramen"],["Ryōtei","dish/ryotei"],["Sashimi","dish/sashimi"],["Sencha","dish/sencha"],["Shizuoka oden","dish/shizuoka-oden"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c9/Tsukudaniphoto.jpg/500px-Tsukudaniphoto.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Jun OHWADA","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tsukudaniphoto.jpg"}},"site/pakistaneats-substack-com":{"t":"site","r":"site/pakistaneats-substack-com","n":"Pakistan Eats","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Recipe sites","sites"]],"sub":"Recipe site, pakistaneats.substack.com","square":true,"suggested":true,"intro":[["Maryam Jillani's newsletter on Pakistani food, recipes and food culture."]],"actions":[{"kind":"out","url":"https://pakistaneats.substack.com","label":"Visit pakistaneats.substack.com"}],"sections":[{"h":"Cuisines it covers","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Pakistani cuisine","ref":"cuisine/pakistani-cuisine","color":"#f0b340","shape":"cuisine","sub":"Pakistan Eats","url":"https://pakistaneats.substack.com/p/pakistani-dishes-for-beginners"}]}]},"site/xawaash-com":{"t":"site","r":"site/xawaash-com","n":"Xawaash","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Recipe sites","sites"]],"sub":"Recipe site, xawaash.com","square":true,"suggested":true,"intro":[["Somali recipe blog started in 2011 by Leila Adde and Abdullahi Kassim in Ontario."]],"actions":[{"kind":"out","url":"https://xawaash.com","label":"Visit xawaash.com"}],"sections":[{"h":"Cuisines it covers","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Somali cuisine","ref":"cuisine/somali-cuisine","color":"#e46a3f","shape":"cuisine","sub":"Xawaash","url":"http://xawaash.com/"}]}]},"cookbook/the-tasmania-pantry":{"t":"book","r":"cookbook/the-tasmania-pantry","n":"The Tasmania Pantry","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Cookbooks","cookbooks"]],"sub":"Cookbook by Eloise Emmett","square":true,"suggested":true,"isbn":"9780994341693",